# PAM configuration for wdm — Fedora and derivatives.
#
# Install as /etc/pam.d/wdm. The rpm package does this; the Arch and Debian
# stacks live in pam.d-wdm and pam.d-wdm.debian.
#
# Same shape as the Arch stack, but Fedora has no system-login: the common
# stack is authselect's system-auth, and session work that only makes sense
# for a real login — pam_lastlog and friends — is split into postlogin, which
# every login-capable service is expected to include after system-auth.
# system-auth is what brings in pam_systemd, which creates the logind session
# and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR that the session inherits; without it most
# desktops will not start. pam_loginuid is not in system-auth, so it is listed
# here the way sddm and gdm list it.
#
# The session stack is bracketed by pam_selinux close/open, as every
# login-capable Fedora service brackets its own. `close` runs first and resets
# any exec context inherited from wdm; `open` computes the context the user's
# session should run in and arms setexeccon(3) for the exec that follows, so it
# has to come before system-auth rather than after — which is how the bracket is
# meant to work, and is not what happens here: see the ponytail: below, which
# also covers pam_loginuid, pam_keyinit and pam_namespace.
#
# pam_keyinit is listed after pam_selinux open and pam_namespace, which is where
# Fedora's own login and sshd stacks put it: the session keyring is created with
# the exec context already computed. Running it earlier is not known to break
# anything, but this file cannot be tested from the machine it is written on, so
# it follows the ordering the distribution ships rather than an improvised one.
#
# ponytail: four modules in this stack are inert as wdm currently forks, and
# they are all of them — everything not named here does work. Each sets
# *per-thread* state, and wdm runs pam_open_session on its PAM thread while
# forking the session from the main one; fork(2) copies only the calling thread,
# so none of it reaches the session:
#
#   pam_selinux open   the armed exec context. A confined login (staff_u,
#                      guest_u, xguest_u) is still unconfined and MCS separation
#                      does not apply, on a distribution shipping SELinux
#                      enforcing.
#   pam_loginuid       loginuid is per-task and inherited from the *forking*
#                      thread, so every graphical session is unattributable in
#                      the audit trail — auditd records it against wdm's own
#                      loginuid rather than the user's. It is `required`, which
#                      makes it look load-bearing; it is not, here.
#   pam_namespace      unshare(CLONE_NEWNS) is per-thread, so a polyinstantiated
#                      /tmp or /home configured in namespace.conf silently does
#                      not apply to the session.
#   pam_keyinit        the joined session keyring.
#
# The modules stay because they are correct for the day that changes. The
# upgrade path is either to fork the session from the PAM thread, or to re-apply
# each of these in the forking thread after pam_open_session returns:
# setexeccon(3), a write to /proc/self/loginuid, unshare(2), and
# keyctl_join_session_keyring(3). The loginuid write is the awkward one — with
# AUDIT_FEATURE_LOGINUID_IMMUTABLE the kernel permits it only once per task and
# refuses any later change, so a thread whose loginuid has already been set
# cannot correct it — which is the strongest argument for moving the fork onto
# the PAM thread instead of patching state up afterwards.

auth        substack    system-auth
-auth       optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so
-auth       optional    pam_kwallet5.so
auth        include     postlogin

account     required    pam_nologin.so
account     include     system-auth

password    include     system-auth

session     required    pam_selinux.so close
session     required    pam_loginuid.so
session     required    pam_selinux.so open
session     optional    pam_namespace.so
session     optional    pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session     include     system-auth
session     include     postlogin
-session    optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
# pam_kwallet5 is started again as of 0.5.0. It was removed in 0.4.0 because it
# forks inside pam_sm_open_session and, on its error path, calls exit() in the
# child rather than exec'ing: wdm held DRM and a live EGL context at that point,
# so the child ran the graphics driver's atexit handlers against GPU state it
# shared with the parent. On NVIDIA that faulted wdm's own channel
# (NVRM: Xid 31 ... name=wdm) and took the compositor down mid-login — the user
# authenticated successfully and then lost the display manager.
#
# That hazard is closed, and not by treating this one module. wdm no longer runs
# PAM in the process that owns the display at all. `wdm --pam-helper` is a
# freshly exec'd copy of wdm's own binary with no graphics driver ever loaded
# into it, and wdm releases the DRM device, the renderer, libinput and the
# libseat session *before* it tells the helper to open the session. So there is
# no EGL context anywhere for a forking module to inherit, whatever it does with
# it — which is the point: any module that forks and exits during open_session
# reproduced this, not just pam_kwallet5.
#
# The two lines go and come back together. pam_kwallet5's auth module does
# nothing but stash the password in a pipe for the session module to read, so
# either both are live or neither is; leaving one behind is work whose result
# nothing collects.
-session    optional    pam_kwallet5.so auto_start
